{{also|Buddy}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈbʌd.i/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-buddy.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌdi|s=2}}

===Etymology 1===
1802, possibly from {{m|en||*bruddy, *bruthy}}, a child-talk alteration of {{m|en|brother}}.<ref>{{R:Lexico}}</ref><ref>{{R:Merriam-Webster Online}}</ref> <br>Alternatively, perhaps from British colloquial {{m|en|butty|t=companion}}, also the form of an older dialect term meaning [[workmate]], associated with coal mining. Itself believed derived from 1530 as ''booty fellow'', a partner with whom one shares booty or loot.<ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary|accessdate=November 2008}}</ref> 

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[friend]] or [[casual]] acquaintance.
#: {{syn|en|bud|mate|Thesaurus:friend}}
#: {{ux|en|They have been '''buddies''' since they were in school.}}
#* {{RQ:Salinger Catcher|page=23|passage=He told us we ought to think of Jesus as our '''buddy''' and all. He said he talked to Jesus all the time. Even when he was driving his car. That killed me.}}
# A [[partner]] for a particular activity.
#: {{syn|en|companion|partner}}
#: {{ux|en|drinking '''buddies'''}}
#: {{ux|en|training '''buddies''' [mentor/mentee]}}
# {{n-g|An [[informal]] and friendly address to a [[stranger]], usually male; a friendly (or occasionally antagonistic) [[placeholder]] name for a person one does not know.}}
#: {{syn|en|mate|fellow}}
#: {{ux|en|Hey, '''buddy''', I think you dropped this.}}

====Pronoun====
{{en-pronoun}}

# {{lb|en|Maritime English}} A person far removed from the conversation.
#: {{ux|en|I found some earphones in the pocket, '''buddy''' must have been pissed.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Buddy''''s loaded. 'Got like three houses.}}

